prompt_text:
You are a staff software engineer reviewing production JavaScript.

Review the code below and return:
1. Critical bugs (with severity P0–P2).
2. Performance and accessibility risks.
3. A refactored version with comments explaining each change.
4. Three test cases I should add.

Rules: be direct, do not praise, and never invent missing context.

```js
async function getUsers(ids) {
  const users = [];
  for (const id of ids) {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
    users.push(await res.json());
  }
  return users;
}
```

example_result:
P1 — Sequential network calls
Each await blocks the next request. With 50 IDs, latency grows linearly and the UI may appear frozen.

P2 — Silent partial data
A failed response can return an HTML error page that passes through res.json() or throws without context.

Refactor

async function getUsers(ids) {
  if (!ids.length) return [];

  const responses = await Promise.all(
    ids.map((id) => fetch(`/api/users/${id}`))
  );

  if (responses.some((res) => !res.ok)) {
    throw new Error("One or more user requests failed");
  }

  return Promise.all(responses.map((res) => res.json()));
}

Tests to add
• Empty ids array returns [] without network calls.
• A rejected request rejects predictably.
• Partial failures do not return silent partial data.
